WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.540
A rat ignores a light touch on its paw.

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.560
Days after a nerve injury, the same touch makes it pull away.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.460
Why?

4
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.690
The rat stands on a wire-mesh floor.

5
00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:16.970
A thin filament presses up against its hind paw.

6
00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:18.870
The paw stays down.

7
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.690
Then comes a spared nerve injury.

8
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.530
Two branches of the nerve to the paw are cut, and one is spared.

9
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.240
Days later, the same filament makes the paw lift.

10
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.110
Allodynia means pain from a stimulus that normally does not hurt.

11
00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.710
A rat cannot tell us, so the paw lift stands in for it.

12
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:44.273
The touches come from filaments of rising stiffness, pressed over repeated trials.

13
00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:48.113
Many labs take the force that lifts the paw half the time.

14
00:00:48.893 --> 00:00:50.333
That is the withdrawal threshold.

15
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:53.227
Plot that threshold over time.

16
00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.987
In sham-operated rats it stays flat.

17
00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.687
In injured rats it falls within days, and stays low.

18
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.700
When a treatment is said to reverse allodynia, it pushes that curve back up, often

19
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:06.700
only for a while.

20
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.540
Two other words matter.

21
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.880
Hyperalgesia is more pain to a stimulus that already hurts.

22
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.400
Spontaneous pain comes with no applied stimulus.

23
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:19.963
Notice what is measured.

24
00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:25.703
Most rodent papers on allodynia report a withdrawal threshold, which is a reflex.

25
00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:27.843
Hold on to that.

26
00:01:28.517 --> 00:01:33.457
So is persistent pain an alarm that never switched off, or a changed nervous system?

27
00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:35.357
Which would you pick?

28
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.360
The answer is both.

29
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.560
After injury, some sensory neurons fire with no stimulus.

30
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.360
So the alarm wiring itself has changed.

31
00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:52.563
Here is the one idea to carry through.

32
00:01:52.563 --> 00:01:57.703
After a nerve injury, pain persists because the rules of the system have changed.

33
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:01.903
First, sensory neurons make signals with no stimulus.

34
00:02:03.016 --> 00:02:06.156
Second, in rats, the dorsal horn loses inhibition.

35
00:02:07.236 --> 00:02:11.436
In a simple model, a light touch that used to fall short can then fire a

36
00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:12.536
pain-signalling neuron.

37
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.630
Third, in rodents, immune cells help hold that change, by routes that differ with

38
00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:19.650
sex and compartment.

39
00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:25.503
Fourth, separate brain circuits set how unpleasant the result is, apart from the

40
00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:26.503
reflex.

41
00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.636
Most of this comes from rodent nerve-injury models.

42
00:02:33.536 --> 00:02:35.576
In people, the evidence is association.

43
00:02:37.716 --> 00:02:42.756
The takeaway: the alarm wiring changed, and so did the rules for what counts as a

44
00:02:42.756 --> 00:02:43.276
pain signal.
